WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.333
Human-in-the-loop checkpoints.

2
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:04.943
Sending, deleting, paying, posting.

3
00:00:04.943 --> 00:00:09.033
Some actions are too consequential for an agent to take alone.

4
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:13.867
A human-in-the-loop checkpoint pauses the agent at a risky step.

5
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:20.333
It shows the human exactly what is about to happen, then resumes cleanly, possibly with edited state.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.333
For agents touching real systems, this is non-negotiable.

7
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:27.933
The reviewer has three responses, not two.

8
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:29.633
There is a pending action.

9
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:31.857
Approve it, and it runs as is.

10
00:00:31.857 --> 00:00:35.000
Edit it, and it runs with the modified arguments.

11
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.215
Or reject it.

12
00:00:36.215 --> 00:00:40.789
The refusal is fed back as an observation, so the agent re-plans.

13
00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:42.900
A rejection is not a dead end.

14
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.867
Here is the part people miss.

15
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.167
The agent proposes a risky call.

16
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:53.567
Its state must be saved at that checkpoint, because you cannot hold a Python process hostage.

17
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:55.767
A manager may click hours later.

18
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.599
Then the agent resumes from the saved state.

19
00:00:58.599 --> 00:01:02.167
LangGraph's checkpointer and interrupt give you this natively.

20
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:04.700
Here is a simple gate.

21
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.567
If the next tool call is on the risky list, we stop and ask.

22
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:13.267
A no becomes an observation, telling the agent to propose something else.

23
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.500
An edit swaps in new arguments.

24
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.133
And only the approved or edited version ever executes.

25
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:21.833
Here is a real example.

26
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:27.933
The hazard alert tool posts to the ops Slack channel, where real people will see it, so it is gated.

27
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:32.292
The agent drafts an alert for a one hundred sixty meter asteroid.

28
00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:37.600
The reviewer sees the exact message and lowers the severity from warning to advisory.

29
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.600
The edited version posts, the edit is recorded in state, and nothing reaches Slack un-reviewed.

30
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:45.591
Let's recap.

31
00:01:45.591 --> 00:01:47.633
Gate the consequential actions.

32
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:50.367
The human can approve, edit, or reject.

33
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:54.000
A rejection is an observation the agent learns from.

34
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.965
And persist state at the checkpoint.

35
00:01:56.965 --> 00:02:00.000
Next up: MCP, the Model Context Protocol.
